Generate pixel art with the Pro model — best quality, several candidates.

This is "Create Image (Pro)". It returns a SET of options in one call rather
than a single image, by the larger side: 64 candidates up to 32px, 49 at
33-35px, 36 at 36-41px, 25 at 42-50px, 16 at 51-64px, 9 at 65-84px, 16 at
85px, 4 at 86-170px, 1 above that. Cost is per call, not per candidate, so a
small canvas is where it pays off.

Unlike the other two text-to-image tools it accepts labelled references:
  create_image_pro(
      description="knight in the same armour",
      reference_images='[{"base64": "<hero png>", "usage": "character base"},
                         {"base64": "<armour png>", "usage": "outfit"}]')

No facing control: nothing here sets which way the subject looks, and a
reference does not either. To turn a picture you already have into 8
facings, use create_object_pro_flash(first_frame_base64=..., n_directions=8).

For a console or palette look (1-bit, Game Boy, PICO-8, NES, any palette from
Lospec) pass `palette` as its hex colours, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856",
"#88c070", "#e0f8d0"], plus `palette_name`: the image is drawn for the
palette and comes back using only those colours. A palette name alone is not
enough - the colours are what the result is put on. `max_colors` caps the
colours in each image (e.g. 6 for an NES-style sprite).

Pick between the three: create_image_pixflux for img2img or isometric;
create_image_pixen for clean small sprites at 1 generation; this one when
quality matters more than cost, or for a palette given as hex colours.

Cost: 10-25 generations for the CALL, never multiplied by the candidate count.
The rule is one step: the sheet is your canvas times the candidates per side,
and you pay that sheet's tier. Square canvases work out to 10 for 16-32, 43-64,
86-128 and 171-256; 15 for 33-42, 65-85, 129-170 and 257-341; 25 above.
Candidates per call, square canvases by side: 64 for 16-33, 49 for 34-35, 36
for 36-41, 25 for 42-51, 16 for 52-65, 9 for 66-85, 4 for 86-171, 1 from 172.
A NON-SQUARE canvas is not the longest side applied to those ladders, and it is
not the area either: both sides are read, and a narrower canvas can fit one more
per side and so land a tier cheaper. 48x72 returns 16 candidates at 10
generations while 72x72 returns 9 at 15, on the same longest side. Verified
non-square costs: 320x180 and 72x56 are 10, 384x216 and 416x176 are 15, 512x288
and 688x384 are 25. For a size not listed, don't extrapolate from the square
ladders — the submit receipt reports the actual cost and candidate count before
anything renders.
Generation time: ~1-10 minutes (async). Poll with get_image(job_id).

exec tool declaration:
```ts
declare const tools: { mcp__pixellab__create_image_pro(args: {
  // What to draw (e.g. 'ornate iron key with a ruby inlay').
  description: string;
  // Canvas height. Max depends on aspect ratio.
  height?: number;
  // At most this many colours in each image (2-64), e.g. 6 for an NES-style sprite. With a palette, they are picked from it.
  max_colors?: number | null;
  // Return subjects on a transparent background. Defaults True — pro is normally used for assets. Set False for scenes and backdrops.
  no_background?: boolean;
  // Target palette, 2 to 256 hex colours as a JSON array, e.g. ["#081820", "#346856", "#88c070", "#e0f8d0"] for Game Boy or ["#000000", "#ffffff"] for 1-bit. The image is drawn for these colours and every pixel of the result is one of them.
  palette?: Array<string> | null;
  // Optional name of the palette (e.g. 'Game Boy', 'PICO-8', 'NES'); naming it helps the look.
  palette_name?: string | null;
  // Internal Game Builder project context; the finished image is linked after generation.
  project_id?: string | null;
  // Up to 4 LABELLED reference images, as a JSON array. Each entry takes a "url" (preferred) OR a "base64": [{"url": "https://...", "usage": "character base"}, {"url": "https://...", "usage": "outfit and armour"}]. The "usage" note tells the model what to take from each image, which is what makes combining several references work. Prefer urls here especially — four inline images in one argument is four times the payload, and MCP clients truncate large arguments. Sizes are read from the images.
  reference_images?: Array<{ [key: string]: unknown; }> | null;
  // Seed for reproducible generation.
  seed?: number | null;
  // Which aspects to take from style_image_base64: any of color_palette, outline, detail, shading. Defaults to all four, or all but color_palette when a palette is given.
  style_copy?: Array<string> | null;
  // One image whose art style (palette, outline, detail, shading) the result should match. Use style_copy to narrow which of those apply.
  style_image_base64?: string | null;
  // Alternative to style_image_base64: an https (or data:) URL to the style PNG. PREFER THIS for anything but a tiny sprite. Give one, not both.
  style_image_url?: string | null;
  // Canvas width. Max depends on aspect ratio (512x512 square, 688x384 for 16:9).
  width?: number;
}): Promise<CallToolResult>; };
```

